## The claim under test is FALSE: a genuine, reproducible occurrence-vanish
case survives the fix

The brief's chosen convention — key on (Y, M, D) taken in EACH value's own
Location — collides two different failure directions:

1. dayOf(rp.NextExpected) always carries UTC (CSV dates).
2. asOfDay = dayOf(asOf) carries asOf's own Location (time.Local on the
   default path).
3. The advance-loop (`!occ.After(asOfDay)`) correctly filters on REAL
   INSTANTS — Location does not affect `After`/`Before`. It is the balance
   walk that is label-based: it walks calendar-day LABELS
   asOfDay+1 .. asOfDay+35 in ASOF's OWN LOCATION, explicitly excluding
   "today" (asOfDay itself, d=0).

For a positive-UTC-offset zone (e.g. Asia/Tokyo, UTC+9), an occurrence whose
UTC-labeled calendar day equals asOf's LOCAL "today" label, but whose real
instant is still in asOf's future (because the occurrence's midnight-UTC
instant lands at 09:00 local, and asOf is earlier than that in local
wall-clock time), passes the instant-based advance-loop filter (it genuinely
is "after asOf") but is written under a label (asOf's own "today") that the
label-based balance walk never visits, since the walk starts at asOfDay+1.
The occurrence is silently dropped — not applied to the balance at all.

Reproduced with a standalone test (built and run under
internal/services/accounts/, then deleted — not left in the tree, confirmed
by `git status --porcelain` before/after):

```go
loc, _ := time.LoadLocation("Asia/Tokyo")
acct := ...Anchor 800.00 on 2026-08-01, LowBalanceThreshold 500.00...
asOf := time.Date(2026, 9, 1, 8, 0, 0, 0, loc)              // 2026-08-31 23:00 UTC (real instant)
yearly := rp("insurance", 400.00, "yearly",
    time.Date(2026, 9, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC),             // 1 real hour AFTER asOf's instant
    leg("usaa", mustDate(2026, 7, 1)))
got, _ := Project(acct, nil, asOf, []models.RecurringPayment{yearly})
```

Result: `Crossing=0001-01-01 00:00:00 +0000 UTC Minimum=800.00 SuggestedTopUp=0.00`
— i.e. the $400 outflow, which genuinely occurs 1 hour after asOf and
comfortably within the 35-day horizon, never registers. The account reports
"healthy, no top-up needed" when a real, imminent debit was scheduled and
silently dropped. Yearly frequency was used specifically to rule out a
same-run second-cycle collision papering over the drop (confirmed with a
monthly variant first: the near occurrence vanished but a coincidental
second-cycle occurrence 30 days later happened to land on a matching label,
masking the loss behind a wrong-but-nonzero Crossing date one full cycle
later than the true first crossing — itself also a defect, a materially
wrong "first crossing" date shown to the user).

This is exactly the attack the dispatch brief asked me to construct
("asOf is Local and NextExpected is UTC and the instants straddle midnight
— does an occurrence land on the wrong day, or get double-counted, or
vanish?"), and it is not a contrived corner case restricted to a single
instant: the vanish window spans up to |UTC offset| hours of every affected
occurrence's due date, for any host whose time.Local differs from UTC by a
positive offset (e.g. all of APAC). The committed pinning test
(TestProject_RecurringAppliesAcrossUTCWriteAndLocalReadLocations) explicitly
avoids this by using dates "17 days apart... so the fixture's outcome is
identical under any TZ" — i.e. the author was aware the interaction is
timing-sensitive and chose a fixture that cannot exercise the boundary,
rather than one that proves the boundary is safe.

Net effect: the fix correctly repairs the 100%-drop defect R12 was opened
for, but the specific keying/walk-boundary design it lands has its own,
narrower but real and reproducible, false-negative case for near-term
occurrences on non-UTC hosts. The stated acceptance claim ("every
time.Now()-derived projection now applies recurring occurrences correctly")
is not true as delivered.

